loop\items\AI4DEV-57\gate2-kimi-sql.incomplete-run.txt:170: - In `complete-signup`, `p_ip` is passed as `callerIp(request)` which may be a hostname or invalid inet — if invalid, the insert fails with invalid input syntax for inet → 400 from PostgREST → status 409 returned. Hmm, `x-forwarded-for` can contain arbitrary client-controlled text. An attacker can send `X-Forwarded-For: garbage` and the insert fails → signup fails. Actually wait: when running behind Supabase local, does the edge runtime set x-forwarded-for? The client can set the header themselves. A malicious or misconfigured client sending a non-IP x-forwarded-for gets a 409 on signup — availability/robustness issue, minor. Actually worse: the failure message would be the DB error "invalid input syntax for type inet" passed through to caller. Minor robustness concern; reportable as a low-severity finding. Is it a genuine defect? callerIp returns the raw header value. If it isn't a valid inet, complete_signup fails on write 4, rolling everything back — the user cannot complete signup until they stop sending that header. It's an edge case; worth a low-severity finding.
loop\items\AI4DEV-57\gate2-kimi-sql.incomplete-run.txt:190: 6. Check (i): they call `sql`select public.complete_signup(...)`` — direct SQL as the postgres superuser via DB_URL. An exception in plpgsql aborts the statement; the whole function is one statement, so atomicity holds. But — important subtlety: plpgsql function with an inner `begin ... exception` block only wraps the accounts insert. The CHECK violation on acknowledgments is not caught → propagates → statement rolled back. Yes.
loop\items\AI4DEV-57\gate2-kimi-sql.incomplete-run.txt:192: Actually wait — one more subtlety on (i): `complete_signup(${...}::uuid, 'ngo', 'Halfway House', '', ${CLIENT_IP}::inet)` — positional args. p_acknowledgment_text_version = '' passes the function's checks (function doesn't check it) and fails the table CHECK. Yes, as the comment says.
loop\items\AI4DEV-57\gate2-kimi-sql.incomplete-run.txt:273: Given genuine uncertainty, this is a legitimate finding with `unverified-runtime-claim: yes`: "IF stock Supabase default privileges grant ALL on new public tables to anon/authenticated/service_role, then (a) the claim that service_role holds no INSERT is false and the F6 'only door' narrative collapses; (b) revoke from PUBLIC on functions doesn't remove per-role default grants. Settle by: after `bun run db reset`, run `select has_table_privilege('service_role','public.accounts','insert'), has_function_privilege('anon','public.complete_signup(uuid,text,text,text,inet)','execute')`."
loop\items\AI4DEV-57\gate2-kimi-sql.incomplete-run.txt:277: Also for tables: "Measured on the replayed database: service_role holds no SELECT and no INSERT on any of these four tables" (comment in complete_signup). And "Left ungranted, a client-key insert into public.accounts fails at the PRIVILEGE layer ('permission denied for table accounts')". These are claimed measurements of the current migration. If true, default privileges in this stack do NOT grant table privileges to those roles (maybe the image changed, or CLI 2.110's local stack differs). I should report the residual risk but with appropriate hedging, marked unverified. Actually — since they claim measurement and I have no counter-evidence beyond my priors, is this a finding at all? The prompt says: report concerns you actually hold, marked unverified. I do hold this concern because the claim contradicts well-known Supabase behavior... but my knowledge may be outdated. I'll report it as one finding, unverified, low-medium severity, with the precise settling query. That seems right and honest.
loop\items\AI4DEV-57\gate2-kimi-sql.incomplete-run.txt:281: **A. `callerIp` / inet cast failure**: A client can send an arbitrary `X-Forwarded-For` header; if it's not a valid inet, `complete_signup`'s 4th insert fails with `invalid input syntax for type inet`, rolling back the entire signup → the user cannot complete signup. The edge function passes the header value unvalidated into an inet parameter. Client-controlled header → self-DOS on signup; also garbage in, and the returned error leaks the raw DB message. Severity: low-medium. Where: `_shared/edge.ts:96-100` + `complete-signup/index.ts:62`. Concrete failure: request with `X-Forwarded-For: not-an-ip` → 409 "invalid input syntax..." and no account created. Note Kong may overwrite x-forwarded-for — in the local stack, Kong appends? The proof script relies on the header reaching the function. If Kong overwrites/prepends the real client address, the recorded ip could be Kong's view — fine. But when the edge runtime is reached, who sets the header? Unverified whether a client-supplied header survives Kong. Kong typically sets X-Forwarded-For itself (trusted IPs depending on config). Anyway the header reaching `callerIp` is used raw; if invalid inet → signup failure. That's a robustness defect worth reporting as low severity. Hmm — but actually is this "the code does what the plan decided"? Plan says record the request IP. The unvalidated cast is an edge-case/error-path concern — squarely in my attack list ("edge cases, error paths, what happens on the unhappy input"). Report it, low severity.
