loop\items\AI4DEV-58\artifacts\gate2-ts-terra.stderr.log:4578: HEAD:loop/items/AI4DEV-57/gate2-kimi-sql.incomplete-run.txt:170:  - In `complete-signup`, `p_ip` is passed as `callerIp(request)` which may be a hostname or invalid inet — if invalid, the insert fails with invalid input syntax for inet → 400 from PostgREST → status 409 returned. Hmm, `x-forwarded-for` can contain arbitrary client-controlled text. An attacker can send `X-Forwarded-For: garbage` and the insert fails → signup fails. Actually wait: when running behind Supabase local, does the edge runtime set x-forwarded-for? The client can set the header themselves. A malicious or misconfigured client sending a non-IP x-forwarded-for gets a 409 on signup — availability/robustness issue, minor. Actually worse: the failure message would be the DB error "invalid input syntax for type inet" passed through to caller. Minor robustness concern; reportable as a low-severity finding. Is it a genuine defect? callerIp returns the raw header value. If it isn't a valid inet, complete_signup fails on write 4, rolling everything back — the user cannot complete signup until they stop sending that header. It's an edge case; worth a low-severity finding.
