    7: Requirement (board item, verbatim): "Every acknowledgment captures the person's name, title and
    8: an attestation of authority. Omitting any field rejects the acknowledgment. The copy prohibits
    9: shared credentials and recommends an organisation email address." Verifies AT-001.19, AT-001.20,
   10: AT-001.39. Ratified AT text: `.taskmaster/docs/acceptance/at-req-001.md` lines 39â€“41. Ratified
   11: requirement guard: `.taskmaster/docs/requirements/req-001.md` line 8.
   12: 
   13: ## The one fact the whole plan rests on
   14: 
   15: The tree has exactly ONE acknowledgment moment: signup completion. Both session kinds
   16: (email/Google and GitHub-established) reach the same deployed function
   17: (`supabase/functions/complete-signup/index.ts`) and the same database function
   18: `public.complete_signup` (recreated with GitHub parameters by
   19: `supabase/migrations/20260809090000_volunteer_github_link_and_imported_profile.sql`, which
   20: dropped the old signature at its line 172). "Every acknowledgment" therefore means: the three
   21: identity fields ride the completion request and land on `public.acknowledgments`.
   22: 
   23: ## Decisions
   24: 
   25: - **A â€” where the fields live.** Three new columns on `public.acknowledgments`: `signer_name`,
   26:   `signer_title`, `authority_attestation`, each `text not null check (length(btrim(...)) > 0)`
   27:   â€” the same belt-and-braces shape `text_version` already has (migration
   28:   `20260808120000_...` line 78). TypeScript names: `signerName`, `signerTitle`,
   29:   `authorityAttestation`.
   30: - **B â€” where omission rejects.** In `validateCompleteSignup`
   31:   (`supabase/functions/_shared/accounts.ts`), the module both the deployed function and the
   32:   loop-tier fixture drive. The three checks go AFTER the existing acknowledgment-text-version
   33:   check, in the order name â†’ title â†’ attestation, one refusal per field, each refusal naming
   34:   its field. Order matters: every already-green test that pins a refusal reason keeps firing
   35:   its own check first â€” AT-001.07's `platform_admin` refusal at `parseAccountType`, AT-001.04's
   36:   GitHub-link refusal, AT-001.01's missing-acknowledgment refusal (`/acknowledgment/i`) at the
   37:   text-version check. Requests in those tests that omit the signer fields never reach the new
   38:   checks.
   39: - **C â€” the attestation is text, not a boolean.** The record stores the statement the person
   40:   affirmed, verbatim and trimmed, exactly as `text_version` stores which text was accepted. A
   41:   `true` in a column captures that something was clicked; the statement captures WHAT was
   42:   attested. The shipped statement lives in the copy module (decision D) and the tests submit it.
   43: - **D â€” the copy is a shipped constant module.** New file
   44:   `supabase/functions/_shared/acknowledgment-copy.ts`:
   45: 
   46:   ```ts
   47:   export const ACKNOWLEDGMENT_IDENTITY_COPY = {
   48:     authorityStatement:
   49:       'I attest that I have the authority to make this acknowledgment for my organisation â€” ' +
   50:       'to bind it, to fund non-refundable model-fuel purchases, and to accept services that ' +
   51:       'carry no SLA.',
   52:     sharedCredentialsProhibition:
   53:       'Shared credentials are prohibited. Every acknowledgment is made by one named person ' +
   54:       'under their own sign-in.',
   55:     orgEmailRecommendation:
   56:       'An organisation email address is recommended for the account that makes acknowledgments.',
   57:   } as const;
   58:   ```
   59: 
   60:   AT-001.20 grades this constant BY DIRECT IMPORT, the same way the suite already imports
   61:   `stubGithubStatsFor` from a shipped module (`a-signup-and-signin.test.ts` line 46). No new
   62:   SUT-contract method: no deployed surface reports copy (the live adapter's own header, point 3,
   63:   refuses the read-the-module-back pattern for `publicSignupAccountTypes`), and unlike that case
   64:   the copy has no behavioural form at any tier â€” its content IS the article. The screen that
   65:   will display it is later UI work; the green claims content, never display (see "what the green
   66:   claims").
   67: - **E â€” the database function changes by drop-and-recreate.** New migration, timestamp after
   68:   the latest existing file: adds the three columns, then drops `public.complete_signup` by its
   69:   exact current signature and recreates it with three appended parameters `p_signer_name text
   70:   default null`, `p_signer_title text default null`, `p_authority_attestation text default
   71:   null`, inserted into the acknowledgment row. `default null` is the rolling-deploy pattern the
   72:   GitHub leaf established (see the long comment at `complete-signup/index.ts` lines 89â€“100);
   73:   enforcement is the columns' constraints â€” a null reaching the insert aborts the whole
   74:   transaction, so no partial signup can exist. The revoke/grant tail is re-stated after the
   75:   recreate, exactly as migration `20260809090000` does, because a drop loses grants.
   76: - **F â€” what integration proves, and what it cannot.** Integration runs on the item's reserved
   77:   database slot 1 and proves the email/Google completion path end to end: deployed function â†’
   78:   database function â†’ row. A GitHub-established session is not obtainable at integration tier
   79:   (AT-001.02/.04/.05 are `capability-pending` there today), so AT-001.19's GitHub-path clause is
   80:   proved at loop tier only, where the fixture drives the same shared validation. This narrowing
   81:   is stated here, in the test body, and in the merge ruling.
   82: - **G â€” one slice.** Three ids, one deliverable, one coherent diff across one migration, one
   83:   shared module, one edge function, two adapters and one test file. The code gate runs once.
   84: - **H â€” not in scope.** No UI. No new edge function. No change to
   85:   `public.has_platform_acknowledgment`, to `create-organization`, or to any D3/D5/D6 id. No
   86:   backfill story for existing rows: no production database exists, integration slots are reset
